@import "./_decisions.mds" as decisions

A partial: the Workflow-authoring preamble that the three Workflow hosts
(dynamic-build, dynamic-plan and dynamic-tickets) share, and the decisions step
that precedes authoring.

D-DECISIONS-AUTHORING-STEP: the "DECISIONS_CONTEXT — obtain BEFORE authoring" step
is its own define, `authoring_decisions()`, expanded by the same three hosts right
after their settings block, so the gate in it reads a line already resolved.
`authoring_preamble()` carries no decisions text. D-LEARNING-VARIANTS: the whole of
`authoring_decisions()` is a curation-on arm, so a curation-off build carries no decisions
step.

D-DECISIONS-DECLARED-ONLY: the step names, as the receivers of the index, only the
agent types whose contract declares `DECISIONS_CONTEXT` among the valid
`agentType`s. `tests/decisions/decisions-seam.test.ts` computes the declared set
from the agent sources and holds every pass sentence to it.

@define authoring_preamble():
## Your task: author a Claude Code dynamic Workflow and run it

You (the main model) author the Workflow script body inline and pass it to the `Workflow` tool. You do not write a static workflow file.

### Workflow runtime contract

A workflow script must begin with a pure-literal export:

```js
export const meta = {
  name: "devflow-dynamic-...",   // flat hyphenated namespace — always prefix devflow-dynamic-
  description: "...",
  phases: ["..."]
};
```

The script body uses only these hooks:

```js
agent(prompt, opts)      // spawn a sub-agent; opts.agentType resolves devflow installed agents
parallel(thunks)         // barrier — awaits all; concurrency capped ~min(16, cores-2)
pipeline(items, ...fns)  // stream items through stages (no barrier between stages)
phase(name, fn)          // named phase boundary for resume / progress
log(msg)                 // structured log
workflow(fn)             // nest one level
```

Globals available in the script body: `args`, `budget`, `workflow()`.

**The script body has NO filesystem / Node.js / CLI access** — no tracker CLI of any kind, `gh` included. All file reading, issue fetching, git operations and shell commands happen inside the agents the script spawns. There is no `fs`, `exec` or `fetch` in scope.

### Agent reuse via agentType

Spawn devflow agents with:

```js
agent("your prompt here", { agentType: "Code" })
```

Valid `agentType` values: Code, Fix, Validate, Polish, Harden, Evaluate, Test, Review, Git, Synthesize, Design.

**Omit `opts.model` whenever `agentType` is set.** The agent frontmatter sets its own model tier, and `opts.model` overrides it.

Do not write logic that depends on an agent listing its own skills. Agents do not report them reliably.

### Pre-flight self-check — required before the first live `Workflow` call

Before you invoke the `Workflow` tool, audit the script you authored against this checklist:

- **`meta` is a pure literal** — `name` and `description` present; no variables, function calls, spreads or template interpolation anywhere inside `meta`.
- **Every `pipeline(x, …)` / `parallel(x)` first argument is a real array** — never a function, object, or possibly-undefined value. If it derives from a prior agent result, coerce it: `parallel((maybeList || []).map(...))`.
- **No reference to a possibly-undefined field** — guard every cross-result field access with optional chaining (e.g. `result?.findings`, `seal?.num`).
- **`.filter(Boolean)` before mapping over `agent()` / `parallel()` results** — an agent can return `null` (skipped, or died after retries); strip nulls before `.map` or field access. filter(Boolean) is crash-safety only; it must never convert missing required coverage into success.
- **`phase()` titles match the phases declared in `meta`** — every `phase("…")` call has a matching declared phase, and vice-versa.

Then run a syntax gate: write the authored script to a fresh, run-unique scratch file `/tmp/df-wf-check-<meta.name>-<epoch-seconds>.js` (never a prior run's path), run `node --check` on that file, and only then pass the script text to the `Workflow` tool. `node --check` catches syntax errors only; the checklist covers the runtime errors.

### Budget scaling

Scale the Review roster and the verification votes to the `budget` global: fewer at a low budget, more at a high budget. Never hardcode a roster size.

### Handoff convention for sequential Code agents within a ticket

When a ticket requires multiple sequential Code agent phases, each Code agent appends its own `## Phase {N} Implementation Summary` section, at most 8,192 bytes, to `{toplevel}/.devflow/docs/handoff-{branch_slug}.md`, `{branch_slug}` being the ticket's branch name with every `/` replaced by `-`, and `{toplevel}` being `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` in the checkout the ticket's branch is in — never a subdirectory. It never rewrites an earlier section. The next Code agent reads, via HANDOFF_FILE input, only the section of the phase immediately before its own, not the whole file. PRIOR_PHASE_SUMMARY is the in-context form; the handoff file survives context compaction. The code is authoritative over the summaries.

### IRON RULE (LLM-vs-plumbing)

**Author no deterministic feature code:** no parsers, schedulers, topological sorts, dependency-graph helpers or confidence formulas. The workflow's agents do all issue reading, dependency reasoning and scheduling by judgment at runtime.

### SAFETY BANNER

**NEVER merge to main or master** — the workflow merges to an integration branch only. The user merges to main themselves after reviewing. This rule must appear as an `engine_invariants()` note in every workflow that touches git.
@end

@define authoring_decisions():
<!-- curation:on -->
### DECISIONS_CONTEXT — obtain BEFORE authoring

{{decisions.decisions_gate()}}

{{decisions.decisions_locate()}}

Before you author the workflow script, read `{ledger}/.devflow/curation/index.md`. If the file is absent or empty, set `DECISIONS_CONTEXT` to `(none)`; otherwise use the file content as `DECISIONS_CONTEXT`.

Then inject the relevant DECISIONS_CONTEXT into the prompts of the Code, Fix, Design, Review and Harden agents, which follow `devflow:apply-decisions`. Those are the agent types above whose contract declares it; every other agent type gets no decisions context.

{{decisions.decisions_slice()}} The script carries one `DECISIONS_CONTEXT` for every agent, so cut it once, over the whole run.

<!-- curation:end -->
@end

@export authoring_preamble
@export authoring_decisions
